WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:02.760
- So, what is the origin of the law?

00:00:02.760 --> 00:00:03.840
Well, we don't know for certain,

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:05.830
but we think it comes out of Mesopotamia.

00:00:05.830 --> 00:00:07.330
No, not Hammurabi's Code,

00:00:07.330 --> 00:00:09.466
that dates from the 18th century BCE.

00:00:09.466 --> 00:00:11.950
Specifically, the city-state of Ur,

00:00:11.950 --> 00:00:15.290
and then ruler Ur-Nammu,
around the year, 2100 BC.

00:00:15.290 --> 00:00:16.580
Now there are some earlier references

00:00:16.580 --> 00:00:21.010
to a set of laws from Urukagina
in the 24th century BCE,

00:00:21.010 --> 00:00:23.980
but the Ur-Nammu code is the
oldest extant set of laws

00:00:23.980 --> 00:00:24.880
that we still have.

00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:26.760
It's written in a set
of if-then statements.

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:28.690
If someone does this, then this happens.

00:00:28.690 --> 00:00:30.900
So not surprisingly, if
a man commits murder,

00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:31.733
he shall be killed.

00:00:31.733 --> 00:00:33.180
If a man commits robbery,
he shall be killed.

00:00:33.180 --> 00:00:34.920
But what's particularly
interesting about this code

00:00:34.920 --> 00:00:37.590
is that unlike the later
Babylonian codes like Hammurabi,

00:00:37.590 --> 00:00:40.470
which purely focused on
retribution, an eye for an eye,

00:00:40.470 --> 00:00:43.230
this creates a set of
monetary compensation

00:00:43.230 --> 00:00:44.370
for certain acts.

00:00:44.370 --> 00:00:46.310
So for example, if you
cut off a man's foot,

00:00:46.310 --> 00:00:48.570
you owe that person 10 shekels of silver.

00:00:48.570 --> 00:00:50.040
If you knock out someone's tooth,

00:00:50.040 --> 00:00:51.900
you owe them two shekels of silver.

00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:53.330
So this is fairly advanced stuff

00:00:53.330 --> 00:00:55.630
in part of the Sumerian
Renaissance of the time.

00:00:55.630 --> 00:00:58.953
And that is, as far as we can
tell, the origin of the law.

